<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sarnath</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sarnath is a site near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Varanasi</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where the Buddha gave his first sermon in the deer park of Isipatana. It became a major monastic centre: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a> raised a pillar there whose lion capital is now India's national emblem, and the Dhamek Stupa dates largely from the Gupta period. The monasteries declined after the 12th century, and the site was excavated from the 19th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarnath.jpg" alt="Sarnath" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yusuke Kawasaki · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Buddhist_monasteries_near_Dhamekh_Stupa_Monument_Site,_Sarnath.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Gupta artistic conventions in sculpture and iconography, seen at <b>Sarnath</b> and in the early cave temples of Udayagiri, were transmitted across Southeast Asia through trade and religious contact, shaping the artistic traditions of Cambodia, Thailand, and Java for centuries after the empire itself had dissolved.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Provinces were governed by satraps (kshatrapa) and great satraps (mahakshatrapa), such as Kharapallana and Vanaspara, named on a Buddhist statue dedicated at <b>Sarnath</b> in Kanishka's third year.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Ashoka's Lion Capital from <b>Sarnath</b>, carved from polished sandstone over 2,000 years ago, is today the national emblem of the Republic of India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>He gave his first teaching to five former companions in the deer park at <b>Sarnath</b> near Varanasi, an event remembered as the first turning of the wheel of the Dharma.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Govindachandra, the most powerful ruler, fought the Palas and Ghaznavid raiders and patronised learning; his queen Kumaradevi endowed a Buddhist monastery at <b>Sarnath</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/gahadavala-dynasty">Gahadavala dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>The Buddha gave his first sermon at nearby <b>Sarnath</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kashi">Kingdom of Kashi</a></li></ul></section>
